WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.833 --> 00:00:04.876
Last year,

00:00:04.876 --> 00:00:08.838
I spent several months photographing
the fast changes across Afghanistan,

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.217
as we approached the 20th anniversary
of the US invasion.

00:00:12.509 --> 00:00:13.677
At the time,

00:00:13.718 --> 00:00:17.097
I wanted this assignment
to be a love letter to a troubled land

00:00:17.138 --> 00:00:19.391
that I had called home for seven years.

00:00:19.849 --> 00:00:23.645
Little did I know that it will become
a goodbye letter to Afghanistan

00:00:23.687 --> 00:00:25.230
as I knew it.

00:00:25.272 --> 00:00:27.357
Along with our brave local colleagues,

00:00:27.399 --> 00:00:29.192
we traveled across Afghanistan

00:00:29.192 --> 00:00:32.320
to look at how the last 20 years
impacted people.

00:00:32.737 --> 00:00:35.532
I was specifically drawn
to hear from the youth

00:00:35.574 --> 00:00:38.034
who grew up after the American invasion

00:00:38.034 --> 00:00:41.121
in an increasingly open society.

00:00:41.705 --> 00:00:46.293
They grew up in a world with Facebook,
Twitter, American movies and TV shows.

00:00:46.668 --> 00:00:49.504
A world of new freedoms and opportunities.

00:00:49.921 --> 00:00:54.968
They also endured war and terrorism,
poverty and dislocation,

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.512
barely surviving
under a corrupt government

00:00:57.512 --> 00:01:00.056
and the perpetual threat
of suicide bombings.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.145
While the youth in countryside
mostly saw themselves either victimized

00:01:05.145 --> 00:01:07.981
or fighting for both sides
of the conflict,

00:01:08.023 --> 00:01:11.318
the youth in urban areas
had more options and dreams.

00:01:11.818 --> 00:01:14.779
Many of the young men and women
that we met with

00:01:14.821 --> 00:01:17.449
were hanging on to the last rays of hope

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.368
as the country was sinking into darkness

00:01:20.368 --> 00:01:24.581
because of the American withdrawal
and Taliban’s imminent takeover.

00:01:24.956 --> 00:01:29.210
For me, confronting this
was the beginning of the end.

00:01:30.211 --> 00:01:31.963
I want to take you on a journey

00:01:32.005 --> 00:01:36.009
in the months leading
to Taliban’s takeover of Afghanistan,

00:01:37.302 --> 00:01:41.473
and give you snapshots of people
as they lived through this transition.

00:01:41.514 --> 00:01:45.268
Last April, I sat down on the cold
cement floor of a prison

00:01:45.268 --> 00:01:46.561
with two teenage boys

00:01:46.603 --> 00:01:49.481
who were incarcerated
for political charges,

00:01:49.522 --> 00:01:51.983
a loose term for helping the Taliban.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.444
14-year-old Mohammad Asif

00:01:54.486 --> 00:01:57.405
had two brothers fighting
on two sides of the conflict,

00:01:57.447 --> 00:02:00.450
one for the Taliban and one for the army.

00:02:00.825 --> 00:02:06.039
He chose to follow the path of the brother
who was 16 when he joined the Taliban,

00:02:06.081 --> 00:02:08.291
and he was killed before he turned 20.

00:02:09.209 --> 00:02:12.837
I asked both boys to imagine
themselves in ten years.

00:02:13.171 --> 00:02:15.423
Mohammad Asif said nothing and shrugged.

00:02:15.465 --> 00:02:17.133
I challenged him to try harder

00:02:17.175 --> 00:02:20.553
and see himself beyond
the soulless cells of the prison.

00:02:21.513 --> 00:02:22.806
It took several tries.

00:02:22.847 --> 00:02:24.683
He eventually responded,

00:02:24.724 --> 00:02:27.185
"I don't know, I'll probably be dead."

00:02:27.936 --> 00:02:30.939
On the same trip,
we visited Karsai Peak,

00:02:30.939 --> 00:02:33.400
which at the time was serving
as the frontline

00:02:33.441 --> 00:02:36.194
for Afghanistan's national army
fighting the Taliban.

00:02:37.028 --> 00:02:39.989
The mountain range was peppered
by dozens of outposts,

00:02:40.031 --> 00:02:42.575
housing soldiers and a militia unit.

00:02:43.118 --> 00:02:45.578
Most fighters were
in their early twenties.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:47.580
Some of them were teenagers.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.500
Almost all of them
hadn't been home in months.

00:02:50.875 --> 00:02:54.295
At the time, the unit was ambushed
almost every night.

00:02:54.337 --> 00:02:57.340
The young men would stay up
all night fighting

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.676
and take turns to nap during the day.

00:03:00.176 --> 00:03:04.764
On July 2, a month and a half
before the Taliban took over Afghanistan,

00:03:04.806 --> 00:03:07.100
they overran Karsai Peak.

00:03:07.142 --> 00:03:10.937
From the militia unit alone
19 men were killed.

00:03:10.979 --> 00:03:13.690
Another 25 were taken hostage.

00:03:14.274 --> 00:03:17.986
All these young men were the legacy
of the American invasion.

00:03:18.903 --> 00:03:20.321
Meet Hafiza.

00:03:20.363 --> 00:03:24.617
The war drove her out of her village
and turned her sons into enemies.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:28.371
The conflict wedged itself
in between her own family

00:03:28.413 --> 00:03:30.790
as her sons took different paths in life.

00:03:31.124 --> 00:03:34.794
Her oldest son joined
an anti-Taliban militia unit,

00:03:34.794 --> 00:03:36.504
another joined the Taliban,

00:03:36.504 --> 00:03:39.215
and her two youngest
were recruited by the army.

00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:44.137
Here she is revealing an open
wound on her throat,

00:03:44.179 --> 00:03:47.432
a wound that doctors believe
is caused by grief.

00:03:48.892 --> 00:03:52.312
During my visit to Afghanistan's
National Institute of Music,

00:03:52.312 --> 00:03:55.648
the first and only music
school in Afghanistan,

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:58.234
I met 17-year-old Sumbul,

00:03:58.276 --> 00:03:59.986
a brilliant violinist.

00:04:00.445 --> 00:04:02.864
She is the symbol of this generation

00:04:02.906 --> 00:04:05.825
that came of age with relative freedom

00:04:05.867 --> 00:04:07.952
and was robbed of it overnight.

00:04:08.912 --> 00:04:10.079
Sumbul’s family

00:04:10.079 --> 00:04:13.208
come from one of the most
inaccessible regions of Afghanistan.

00:04:13.249 --> 00:04:16.544
Her father was kidnapped
several times by the Taliban,

00:04:16.544 --> 00:04:19.380
being pressured to pull [his] daughters
out of music school.

00:04:20.298 --> 00:04:21.841
After the fall of Kabul,

00:04:21.883 --> 00:04:24.469
Taliban fighters raided and ANIM school.

00:04:24.469 --> 00:04:26.262
Instruments were destroyed.

00:04:26.262 --> 00:04:27.847
On August 26,

00:04:27.889 --> 00:04:30.850
Sumbul and her uncle
were outside of the airport

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:34.187
among thousands of desperate
Afghans trying to flee

00:04:34.229 --> 00:04:37.023
when a suicide bomb
ripped through the crowd.

00:04:38.024 --> 00:04:40.485
Sumbul survived the attack,

00:04:40.485 --> 00:04:44.239
but her dream of performing
inside Afghanistan vanished

00:04:44.239 --> 00:04:47.992
like 170 people who died
all around her that day.

00:04:49.786 --> 00:04:51.955
Over the course of that final summer,

00:04:51.996 --> 00:04:55.291
we watched as the country rapidly
fell into the hands of Taliban.

00:04:55.625 --> 00:04:59.712
One of my colleague's tasks was to keep
track of the fallen district.

00:05:00.088 --> 00:05:02.131
Another to count the fallen men.

00:05:02.715 --> 00:05:08.137
We watched in disbelief as 20 years
of progress in women's rights, education,

00:05:08.179 --> 00:05:09.848
freedom of expression

00:05:09.889 --> 00:05:12.600
disappeared in 20 days.

00:05:13.601 --> 00:05:16.855
On August 15, the day
that Taliban took over Kabul,

00:05:16.855 --> 00:05:18.606
I left the house at 4am

00:05:18.648 --> 00:05:21.651
and headed to the airport
to photograph Afghans

00:05:21.693 --> 00:05:25.697
who were desperately trying to leave
in anticipation of Taliban's victory.

00:05:26.114 --> 00:05:29.284
By noon, Taliban fighters
were marching on the streets,

00:05:29.284 --> 00:05:30.451
and by early evening,

00:05:30.493 --> 00:05:32.704
they had taken over
the presidential palace.

00:05:33.162 --> 00:05:36.916
It was one of the most difficult decisions
of my life to leave that day.

00:05:37.292 --> 00:05:39.335
As we were dashing to the airport,

00:05:39.377 --> 00:05:43.131
hundreds of people came to the street,
running in every direction.

00:05:43.673 --> 00:05:46.092
I will never forget
the panic on their faces.

00:05:47.051 --> 00:05:53.016
I myself was grappling with immense guilt
for leaving and being able to leave.

00:05:54.225 --> 00:05:55.685
I was also heartbroken,

00:05:55.685 --> 00:05:57.812
thinking that I could never return,

00:05:57.854 --> 00:06:00.273
return to this home that has shaped me,

00:06:00.315 --> 00:06:03.109
not only as a photographer,
but a human being.

00:06:04.611 --> 00:06:08.197
Many Afghans have been devastated
and overwhelmed with grief and fear

00:06:08.239 --> 00:06:09.407
since the fall.

00:06:09.449 --> 00:06:11.868
I recently caught up
with Sumbul and Hafiza

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.203
to see where they ended up after the fall.

00:06:14.203 --> 00:06:19.000
Sumbul is healing her scarred soul
while pursuing her dream in Europe.

00:06:19.042 --> 00:06:23.212
Hafiza had gone back to her village
to spend some time with her sons.

00:06:24.505 --> 00:06:26.674
The wound has not healed yet.

00:06:27.216 --> 00:06:29.636
Yet I have to hang on to hope.

00:06:29.677 --> 00:06:33.640
Hope for a country with open wounds
that is struggling to heal.

00:06:33.681 --> 00:06:34.849
Thank you.

00:06:34.849 --> 00:06:37.769
(Applause)

00:06:44.192 --> 00:06:46.235
Shoham Arad: I have a couple of questions.

00:06:46.235 --> 00:06:48.321
I'm a little emotional,
it's not a big deal.

00:06:48.821 --> 00:06:51.074
You've gone back
to Afghanistan since you left.

00:06:51.699 --> 00:06:53.910
Can you tell us
what it feels like right now?

00:06:53.952 --> 00:06:55.995
Kiana Hayeri: The situation is very grim.

00:06:57.413 --> 00:06:59.916
I guess war has ended, but at a cost.

00:07:00.458 --> 00:07:02.752
But poverty is shooting out the roof,

00:07:02.794 --> 00:07:04.128
people are hungry,

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:08.091
girls are banned from going to school,
girls in high school.

00:07:08.132 --> 00:07:10.009
Women are stripped from their jobs.

00:07:10.718 --> 00:07:11.928
It's very grim.

00:07:14.097 --> 00:07:17.183
SA: I know you love this country a lot.

00:07:17.934 --> 00:07:20.561
What's one thing you want
people to know or think about

00:07:20.561 --> 00:07:22.313
when they think about Afghanistan?

00:07:23.189 --> 00:07:25.733
KH: There are many Afghans
arriving abroad,

00:07:25.775 --> 00:07:28.152
in the West, in America, in Canada.

00:07:28.653 --> 00:07:31.114
Open your doors to them,
they've been through a lot,

00:07:31.155 --> 00:07:32.407
they've been traumatized,

00:07:32.407 --> 00:07:34.993
they've been stripped
from everything they had,

00:07:34.993 --> 00:07:36.160
even their identities.

00:07:36.202 --> 00:07:38.454
Welcome them in [your] homes,
take their hands,

00:07:38.496 --> 00:07:41.124
help them to settle,
make them feel at home.

00:07:41.916 --> 00:07:43.418
Also, get involved if you want.

00:07:43.459 --> 00:07:45.586
There are organizations
that are doing stuff,

00:07:45.586 --> 00:07:48.006
especially for girls education
back in Afghanistan.

00:07:48.006 --> 00:07:50.967
Get involved, donate money,
volunteer your time,

00:07:51.009 --> 00:07:53.136
either with people who are arriving here

00:07:53.177 --> 00:07:55.096
or back in Afghanistan.

00:07:55.096 --> 00:07:56.556
SA: Thanks, Kiana.

00:07:56.556 --> 00:07:57.724
KH: Thank you.

00:07:57.765 --> 00:07:59.600
(Applause)

